# BookHop — English string inventory.
#
# Owner: frontend-developer (TASKS §1). EVERY student-facing string in the
# product is here, including the ones that live in `web/forms.py` and
# `web/views.py` rather than in a template — and that is the point of the file.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS WHEN ENGLISH NEEDS NO TRANSLATION.
# `tests/unit/test_forbidden_copy.py` (THREAT-MODEL §6.1(a)) greps "every
# template and .po file". A string in a .py file is in neither, so copy in
# `forms.py` would escape the deny-list entirely. Listing every string here is
# what puts it back in scope. **A string added to a .py file without an entry
# here is outside the control** — backend should assert that every pgettext
# context key in `web/**` appears in this catalogue. Recommended, not built.
#
# WHY THE KEY IS THE msgctxt AND NOT THE msgid.
# COPY-DECK keys by `run.why_email`, and THREAT-MODEL §6.1(b)'s REVIEWED.toml
# example keys its hashes the same way. But this machine has no gettext
# toolchain — no xgettext, no msgfmt — so a symbolic msgid would render as the
# literal string "run.why_email" on every English page. Putting the English in
# the msgid and the deck key in the msgctxt gives both: English ships with no
# compiled catalogue at all (pgettext falls back to the msgid), and the
# manifests still key on the stable deck key rather than on English wording
# that may change.
#
# NOT COMPILED. There is no .mo file and nothing on this machine can make one.
# English is served by msgid fallback. The Tamil catalogue is therefore written
# and **unserved**, which is exactly the state the gate intends.
msgid ""
msgstr ""
"Project-Id-Version: BookHop S1\n"
"Language: en\n"
"MIME-Version: 1.0\n"
"Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8\n"
"Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit\n"
"Plural-Forms: nplurals=2; plural=(n != 1);\n"

